{
  "case_id": "2019-17-1268",
  "document_hash": "271db7adc86c6cec95537eea3c3abe2827c5ddfd74439f5701ac574a3c43636b",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2019-17-1268",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "271db7adc86c6cec95537eea3c3abe2827c5ddfd74439f5701ac574a3c43636b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-271db7adc86c-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 2,
        "start": 2
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 11:10 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2019-17-1268",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "271db7adc86c6cec95537eea3c3abe2827c5ddfd74439f5701ac574a3c43636b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-271db7adc86c-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. McGILL: Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: Sudan provided Al Qaeda with a safe haven and vital material support enabling it to carry out the embassy bombings, killing 224 people and wounding thousands more. In retaliation, President Clinton sent 13 cruise missiles into Khartoum. But to impose punitive damages, Sudan argues, somehow would violate principles of fundamental fairness embodied in the Landgraf presumption."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2019-17-1268",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "271db7adc86c6cec95537eea3c3abe2827c5ddfd74439f5701ac574a3c43636b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-271db7adc86c-0000-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "In 1996 Congress enacted the Flatow amendment which, which explicitly provided for punitive damages for acts of state-sponsored terrorism. But still Sudan continued to harbor Al Qaeda. Sudan had ample opportunity to conform its primary conduct to U.S. law. It just chose not to. And that's not particularly surprising, because Sudan is a foreign government and foreign governments generally are not guided in their policymaking by changes to U.S. law."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2019-17-1268",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "271db7adc86c6cec95537eea3c3abe2827c5ddfd74439f5701ac574a3c43636b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-271db7adc86c-0000-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 10,
        "start": 10
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: Mr. McGill, is it really right that it does -- all the rest of it does apply retroactively? And I mean that in this sense, that under the old statutory scheme, 1605(a)(7), wouldn't a person have been able to get compensatory damages and all the other damages anyway? So that the only new thing that was added by way of this creation of a cause of action is the punitive damages. So I guess that would be a reason to think of the punitives, separate and apart from all the rest, as retroactive."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2019-17-1268",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "271db7adc86c6cec95537eea3c3abe2827c5ddfd74439f5701ac574a3c43636b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-271db7adc86c-0000-0007",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: Ms. Ross, I'm -- I'm trying to process everything you've just said --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2019-17-1268",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "271db7adc86c6cec95537eea3c3abe2827c5ddfd74439f5701ac574a3c43636b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-271db7adc86c-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Your colleague\nsaid that we don't have to get into the\nLandgraf/Altmann issue, that we can just see the\nplain language of the statute and say\nwhatever -- however we look at it through the\nLandgraf lens or the Altmann lens, Congress was\nclear: Punitive damages apply retroactively in\nthis case.\nDo you agree with that proposition?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2019-17-1268",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "271db7adc86c6cec95537eea3c3abe2827c5ddfd74439f5701ac574a3c43636b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-271db7adc86c-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 52,
        "start": 52
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Is that a new argument? I thought -- are you saying that the federal cause of action does not apply retroactively at all?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2019-17-1268",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "271db7adc86c6cec95537eea3c3abe2827c5ddfd74439f5701ac574a3c43636b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-271db7adc86c-0002-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 54,
        "start": 54
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Let's assume that this had been filed earlier than the amendment, that it was -- that it goes back to or it was timely filed before. Would you concede that 1605A(c) would make punitive damages retroactive as to that class of cases?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2019-17-1268",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "271db7adc86c6cec95537eea3c3abe2827c5ddfd74439f5701ac574a3c43636b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-271db7adc86c-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 59,
        "start": 59
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Are they -- I know one is a limitation, statute of limitation question, which is an affirmative defense, not a jurisdictional issue."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2019-17-1268",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "271db7adc86c6cec95537eea3c3abe2827c5ddfd74439f5701ac574a3c43636b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-271db7adc86c-0002-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 62,
        "start": 62
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "A related action is -- is one which is -- where there is a -- a case that has been timely commenced under the old terrorism exception, 1605(a)(7), and that's the Owens case, filed in 2001, then any other action -- turn the page -- arising out of the same act or incident may be brought under 1605A."
    },
    {
